    <h1>How to Evaluate a Dried Chilli Supplier: A Qualification Checklist</h1>
    <p class="lead">
      A supplier who says "we have HACCP" has described their system; a supplier who can show the lot
      COA has reduced your risk. This checklist separates the two.
    </p>

    <div class="takeaways">
      <strong>Short answer:</strong> Evaluate a chilli supplier on four things — food-safety
      certification, a lot-specific COA, traceability from growing base to lot, and verification by
      sample or factory audit. The biggest red flag is no lot COA.
    </div>

    <h2 style="margin-top:32px;">The four things to check</h2>
    <ol>
      <li><strong>Food-safety certification</strong> — HACCP, ISO 22000 or equivalent, and any
      destination-specific certificates (organic, phytosanitary).</li>
      <li><strong>Lot-specific COA</strong> — a certificate of analysis for the lot you are buying,
      showing measured SHU, ASTA, moisture, microbial, pesticide and mycotoxin values.</li>
      <li><strong>Traceability</strong> — a chain from growing base to finished lot, with retained
      samples and a recall process.</li>
      <li><strong>Verification</strong> — a sample against spec, and a factory audit for large
      programmes.</li>
    </ol>

    <h2 style="margin-top:32px;">The qualification checklist</h2>
    <ul>
      <li>Certificate: current, in the supplier's name, covering the products you buy.</li>
      <li>COA: lot-specific, not a range copied from a catalogue.</li>
      <li>Mycotoxin and pesticide screens: against your target market's limits (see
      <a href="/guides/food-safety-quality/">food safety</a>).</li>
      <li>Traceability: growing-base to lot, with retained samples.</li>
      <li>Sample: matches the specification you wrote (see
      <a href="/guides/paprika-chilli-buying-guide/">how to specify</a>).</li>
      <li>Audit: production chain and food-safety controls (see
      <a href="/quality-certifications/">our quality &amp; certifications</a>).</li>
    </ul>

    <h2 style="margin-top:32px;">Red flags that signal risk</h2>
    <ul>
      <li><strong>No lot COA</strong> — only a generic certificate or a catalogue range.</li>
      <li><strong>No traceability</strong> — cannot link the lot to a growing base.</li>
      <li><strong>Vague specifications</strong> — "hot chilli" instead of a variety and SHU window.</li>
      <li><strong>Certificate in a different name</strong> — a broker reselling someone else's cert.</li>
    </ul>

    <h2 style="margin-top:32px;">The Author's Take</h2>
    <p>
      <strong>Position:</strong> In my view, the single most useful test of a chilli supplier is simple:
      can they produce a lot-specific COA for the lot you are actually buying?
    </p>
    <p>
      <strong>Reasoning:</strong> Aflatoxins and Salmonella are invisible, so the only evidence that
      counts is a test number tied to your lot. A supplier who offers a system description instead of a
      test result has not reduced your risk — they have described it. Certification proves the system;
      the COA proves the lot. Buyers who demand both, and a sample, rarely get burned.
    </p>
    <p>
      <strong>Disclosure:</strong> This is my editorial view as a dried-chilli supplier's technical team,
      not a contractual or regulatory statement.
    </p>

